        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> According to the original implementation, to disable indentscope in a specific buffer, only the blacklist method can be used (by setting b:miniindentscope_disable=true). It is difficult to achieve under the above circumstances.\r\n> \r\n> So I want to enable indentscope by using the whitelist. To achieve this function, it is only necessary to modify the global and local usage logic of the `miniindentscope_disable` variable to be consistent with the behavior of neovim.\r\n\r\nThe present approach can work both for [selective automated disabling](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/87fc38a8a2b8d498cecb021d057a38ea2e9f5a79/doc/mini.txt#L345-L347) (i.e. blocklist) and [selective automated enabling](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/87fc38a8a2b8d498cecb021d057a38ea2e9f5a79/doc/mini.txt#L349-L355) (i.e. allowlist). The latter can be done by setting `vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = true` for *all* buffers while excluding only those which user wants to enable.\r\n\r\nAs far as I understand, having no way to automatically disable inside help window of 'nvim-tree/nvim-tree' is fully equivalent to having now way to automatically enable it. Both of them rely on *some* approach to detect when the buffer for the help window is created.\r\n\r\nThe current approach of treating `g:minixxx_disable` and `b:minixxx_disable` (i.e. [disable if at least one is `v:true`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/87fc38a8a2b8d498cecb021d057a38ea2e9f5a79/doc/mini.txt#L318)) is more targeted towards the use case of \"set `vim.g.minixxx_disable` to unconditionally disable certain buffer *everywhere*\". I.e. even if some buffer-local disable variable is set to `false`.\r\n\r\nI can see the merit of respecting actually set buffer-local variable over global one, but that would essentially mean that buffer-local variable has three possible states (`nil`, `false`, `true`) which slightly conflicts with how Lua treats both `nil` and `false` as falsy. If implementing this approach today, I *might* consider doing it the \"three state way\", but changing it right now feels more like an unnecessary chore (especially as I don't see a use case which makes it possible while impossible under current approach).\r\n\r\nSo I am going to close this as not planned.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nThe proper approach in this particular 'nvim-tree/nvim-tree' help window use case I *think* is to create custom `g?` mapping that would toggle help window and then set `vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = false`. For example, by following [this example](https://github.com/nvim-tree/nvim-tree.lua/blob/70825f23db61ecd900c4cfea169bffe931926a9d/doc/nvim-tree-lua.txt#L217-L243).",
