        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> The present approach can work both for [selective automated disabling](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/87fc38a8a2b8d498cecb021d057a38ea2e9f5a79/doc/mini.txt#L345-L347) (i.e. blocklist) and [selective automated enabling](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/87fc38a8a2b8d498cecb021d057a38ea2e9f5a79/doc/mini.txt#L349-L355) (i.e. allowlist). The latter can be done by setting vim.b.miniindentscope_disable = true for all buffers while excluding only those which user wants to enable.\r\n\r\nThe issue is that many plugins may create scratch buffers and not set filetype in many cases (not limited to nvim-tree), which makes it quite difficult to automatically set variables locally within buffers.\r\n\r\nThe essence is that the `mini.indentscope` plugin generally works in normal buffers, which will set basic information such as filetype, and for those scratch buffers (which often have many strange behaviors, such as no filetype, ignoring all autocmds), `b:miniindentscope_disable` must be set to be disabled, which is quite difficult to achieve in practice.\r\n\r\nTherefore, I think it is necessary to modify the working method of this variable to be consistent with the local option behavior of neovim.",
